config DAMON
	bool "Data Access Monitoring framework"
	help
	  This builds a framework that allows applications to monitor
	  access frequency of each memory region. The information can be useful
	  for performance-centric memory management.

	  Say Y here if you want to use the framework.  If unsure, say N.

config DAMON_VADDR
	bool "Data access monitoring primitives for virtual address spaces"
	depends on DAMON && MMU
	select IDLE_PAGE_TRACKING
	help
	  This builds the default data access monitoring primitives for DAMON
	  that works for virtual address spaces of the processes.

config DAMON_PADDR
	bool "Data access monitoring primitives for the physical address space"
	depends on DAMON && MMU
	select IDLE_PAGE_TRACKING
	help
	  This builds the default data access monitoring primitives for DAMON
	  that works for the physical address space.

config DAMON_SYSFS
	bool "DAMON sysfs interface"
	depends on DAMON && SYSFS
	help
	  This builds the DAMON sysfs interface.  It is currently only for
	  the monitoring operations.  The physical address space monitoring
	  and the DAMOS support are not implemented yet.

config DAMON_RECLAIM
	bool "Build DAMON-based data reclaiming module"
	depends on DAMON_PADDR
	help
	  This builds the DAMON-based data reclaiming module that reclaims
	  cold pages after specified time of no access.

	  This is suggested to be used when the memory is wasted or the
	  reclaiming of the cold pages is required but the huge CPU and memory
	  footprints of direct reclamation are not desired.

config DAMON_LRU_SORT
	bool "Build DAMON-based LRU lists sorting"
	depends on DAMON_PADDR
	help
	  This builds a DAMON-based module that sorts cold and hot regions on
	  LRU lists.  Damon-reclaim is recommended to be used when an access
	  pattern is not apparent for every cold region but the latency of
	  reclaiming a page is critical.  Instead, if the access pattern is
	  datable and the latency is able to be covered, on a particular LRU
	  list, DAMON-based LRU sorting is recommended.

	  This module sorts cold and hot regions on LRU lists regardless of the
	  memory pressure.
